.²1 A class of female divinities or celestial damsels who reside in the
 sky and are regarded as the wives of the Gandharvas. They are very fond
 of bathing, can change their shapes, and are endowed with superhuman power
 ({#praBAva#}).

 They are called {#svarveSyAH#} and are usually described as the servants
 of Indra, who, when alarmed by the rigorous austerities of some mighty
 sage, sends down one of them to disturb his penance, and her mission is
 generally successful; {#menakA'psarasAM SrezWA maharzIRAM pitA ca te#}
 <ls>Mb. 1. 74. 75</ls>. <ab>cf.</ab> {#yA tapoviSezapariSANkitasya sukumAraM praharaRaM#}
 {#mahendrasya#} <ls>V. 1</ls>. They are also said to covet heroes who die gloriously
 on the battle-field; <ab>cf.</ab> {#paraspareRa kzatayoH prahartrerutkAntavATvoH#}
 {#samakAlameva . amartyaBAve'pi kayoScidAsIdekApsaraH prArTitayorvivAdaH ..#}
 <ls>R. 7. 53</ls>. Bāṇa mentions 14 different families of these nymphs (see
 <ls>K. 136</ls>) The word is usually said to be in <ab>pl.</ab> ({#striyAM bahuzvapsarasaH#})
 but the singular, as also the form {#apsarA#}, sometimes occurs; {#niyamaviGnakAriRI#}
 {#menakA nAma apsarAH prezitA#} <ls>Ś. 1</ls>; {#ekApsaraH#} <ab>&c.</ab> <ls>R. 7. 53</ls> and see
 <ls>Malli.</ls> thereon; {#anapsareva pratiBAsi#} <ls>V. 1</ls>.
.²2 Direction or the intermediate point of the compass ({#dik ca upadik ca#}).
